---
// The home page: the trunk of the tracker (design/tracker-pattern.md, section 1, ruled by John
// 2026-10-16). The first screen is built like The Belt Equation's (the ruled reference): two
// columns on a desktop, stacked on a phone. Left, in the pattern's order (rule 1.3): the eyebrow;
// the thing held, in the largest type on the site, which here is the chance that we are alone in
// the galaxy, with the middle estimate and the chance we are alone in the observable universe in
// smaller type beneath it (leading with the chance-alone figure is the master session's call on
// 2026-10-16, within section 8's allowance for what a tracker holds at the top); the question as
// the title; one paragraph for a newcomer; the width of the argument in one line; the last-checked
// strip; two buttons. Right: the equation line as the trunk's picture, each term a link to its
// piece's page with the range this site uses under it (rule 2.4). No control on the first screen:
// the calculator lives on the equation page (rule 1.4). Until the run exists, every number reads
// "not computed yet" in words, never a placeholder digit. The big number, the strip and the
// equation line are copied from The Belt Equation's home page (site/src/pages/index.astro there).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { records, longDate, plainNumber, percent, rangeText, plural, symbolHtml, medianWords } from '../lib/records';

const head = records.headline;
const c = records.counts;
const factors = records.factors;
const written = factors.filter((f) => f.exists).length;
const width = head.available && head.optimist !== undefined && head.pessimist !== undefined
  ? { optimist: head.optimist, pessimist: head.pessimist }
  : null;
const NOT_YET = 'not computed yet';
---

<Base>
  <section class="home-hero">
    <div class="home-hero-main">
      <p class="eyebrow">A public explainer and tracker of the Fermi Paradox</p>
      <p class={`big-number ${head.available ? '' : 'pending'}`}>{head.available ? percent(head.alone_galaxy) : NOT_YET}</p>
      <p class="big-claim-line">The chance there is no other detectable civilisation in our galaxy right now.</p>
      <p class="under-number">
        <span>{head.available ? <><strong>{plainNumber(head.median)}</strong> detectable civilisations in our galaxy at the middle of the spread: {medianWords(head.median).replace(/^about one detectable civilisation/, 'one')}</> : <><strong>{NOT_YET}</strong> detectable civilisations in our galaxy at the middle of the spread</>}</span>
        <span><strong>{head.available ? percent(head.alone_universe) : NOT_YET}</strong> the chance we are alone in the observable universe</span>
      </p>
      <h1 class="big-claim">Where is everybody?</h1>
      <p class="big-sub">
        The physicist Enrico Fermi asked that over lunch in 1950: the galaxy is old enough and big enough that someone should have shown up by now, and nobody has. This site takes the question apart and keeps every serious answer to it on a page of its own. The number above is worked out from seven pieces, each with a range drawn from what has been published, and is never typed in.
      </p>
      <p class="width-line">
        {width
          ? <>Take every optimistic published estimate and you get <strong>{plainNumber(width.optimist)}</strong> civilisations in the galaxy; every pessimistic one and you get <strong>{plainNumber(width.pessimist)}</strong>. That gap is the argument.</>
          : <>Take every optimistic published estimate and you get one number; every pessimistic one and you get another. That gap is the argument, and both numbers are {NOT_YET}.</>}
      </p>
      <p class="scan-strip">
        <span class="strip-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>
          No weekly reading of the records against the news has run yet. The records were last changed on {longDate(records.exported_on)}: {written} of the seven pieces written up, {plural(c.explanations, 'explanation', 'explanations')}, {plural(c.publications, 'paper logged', 'papers logged')}.
          {' '}<a href="/story/">See the story so far</a>.
        </span>
      </p>
      <div class="ctas">
        <a class="btn" href="/equation/">Why so uncertain?</a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/method/#who">Who is asking</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="trunk" id="pieces">
      <p class="trunk-label">The seven pieces the number comes from</p>
      <p class="trunk-eq" aria-label="The equation">
        <span class="trunk-n">N</span><span class="op">=</span>
        {factors.map((f, i) => (
          <>
            <a class={`trunk-term ${f.range ? '' : 'missing'}`} href={f.path} title={f.name}>
              <span class="trunk-sym" set:html={symbolHtml(f.symbol)}></span>
              <span class="trunk-range">{f.range ? rangeText(f.range, f.unit, true) : 'no range yet'}</span>
            </a>
            {i < factors.length - 1 && <span class="op">×</span>}
          </>
        ))}
      </p>
      <ul class="trunk-key">
        {factors.map((f) => (
          <li><span class="sym" set:html={symbolHtml(f.symbol)}></span><a href={f.path}>{f.name}</a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="trunk-foot">N is how many civilisations in our galaxy could be detected right now. Each piece is a range; the number at the top comes from multiplying the seven many thousands of times with a random pick from each. <a href="/equation/#calculator">Try the numbers yourself</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="explanations">
    <div class="shead">
      <h2>Every serious explanation for the silence</h2>
      <span>{plural(c.explanations, 'so far', 'so far')}, in three families</span>
    </div>
    <div class="families-home">
      {records.families.map((fam) => (
        <a class="family-card" href={`/explanations/#${fam.key}`}>
          <h3>{fam.name}</h3>
          <p>{fam.blurb}</p>
          <span class="count">{plural(fam.count, 'explanation', 'explanations')}</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section moves">
    <div class="move">
      <p class="kicker">What would move it</p>
      <p>A planet with signs of life in its air, or a survey that finds none on hundreds of them. The two pieces we know least about are whether life starts at all and whether it becomes intelligent; everything else is a detail beside those. Each piece's page says what discovery would narrow its range.</p>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/equation/">See the seven pieces</a>
    </div>
    <div class="move">
      <p class="kicker">What has changed</p>
      <p>Every paper and discovery that bears on the numbers is logged with its date, which piece it touches, and whether it moved a range. The log reaches back to the founding papers of the 1960s and forward to whatever is published next.</p>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/story/">Read the story so far</a>
    </div>
    <div class="move">
      <p class="kicker">Think I'm wrong?</p>
      <p>Good. Every piece has a page, every estimate a source, and every explanation a statement of what would strengthen or weaken it. Name a missing explanation, argue a range with a source, or point out an error, and the change lands with a dated line.</p>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="/disagree/">Disagree with something</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="dim home-meta">
    Records as of {longDate(records.exported_on)}. Everything on this site is built from a public repository, <a href="https://github.com/jfredson/quiet-galaxy" rel="noopener">on GitHub</a>, where every number carries its source and every change its date.
    <span class="sep">|</span><a href="/method/">How the numbers are worked out</a><span class="sep">|</span><a href="/library/">Books and films that take the question seriously</a>
  </p>
</Base>
